G="The loss of a child"
E=[(G,139,"This is what communion looks like in real life—body, heart, mind, and soul fully integrated in love.\n","This is what communion looks like in real life—body, heart, mind, and soul fully integrated in love.\n\nAnd this is why losing a child is every parent's worst nightmare. No one questions it, not even the man who believes in no God at all. A mother and father who give a child life share, as no other human bond allows, in God's own work of creating: \"Called to give life, spouses share in the creative power and fatherhood of God\" (CCC 2367). They pour themselves into that life, body, heart, mind, and soul, and a communion is born between them and their child unlike any other they will know. So when a child dies, the grief has no measure, and that grief is itself a witness. We do not mourn like this for what we were never one with. The depth of the wound shows the depth of the communion, and no parent who has buried a child needs to be told it was real. Nor is it lost: as we will see, it is a communion death cannot sever.\n")]
